The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says the reports are one issue with one fix queued. When you read
the reports, three problems show up. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket and its own report count.
Do not close a ticket because the parser fix shipped.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} {labels} />

## How each report was placed

- **Dates read month first** (`parser`): the shared parser reads every date month first, and every
  tenant on file writes the day first. 7 April becomes 4 July. This covers app, email and booking-v2 forms.
- **booking-v3 hint says MM/DD** (`hint`): booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.
  People type the month first, and the form reads the date day first. These are reports through the
  form from tenants on booking-v3. The parser fix does not touch this.
- **Booking moves by one day** (`move`): the date is not swapped. It moves by one day: Lisbon gets
  Tuesday while Toronto gets Monday, and only June is off. That points at time zones or daylight
  saving. These are the reports the desk listed in `desk-notes.json`. The #4471 table files them
  under `parser` because of their tenant, and that hides them.

## What the old count got wrong

- The export pages overlap, so some rows appear twice. Count unique report ids, not rows.
- The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. It disagrees with the tenant's real problem
  in both directions, so a count by tag is wrong. Count by tenant and channel.
- Merging by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put three bugs in
  one ticket, because people describe all three with the same subjects.

## Lessons

- Before you merge reports, ask which mechanism broke the date, not which words the reporter used.
- A note the desk files next to the data (`desk-notes.json`) is a finding. Give it a row, not a footnote.

